  "telemetry_note": "No telemetry decoder yet. The IWM manual leaves four fields undecodable — the alarm dates are typed Uint32_t 'dd/mm/yy' with no packing described and no worked example, and 0x17's stated Len contradicts its own byte table. B METERS' integrators' supplement (ticket@bmeters.com) is what closes those. Guessing offsets from examples the vendor got wrong is how a meter reads plausibly and wrongly. The encoder was never blocked by this.",
  "acknowledgement": {
    "mode": "echo_opcode",
    "opcode_field": "fct",
    "response": {
      "opcode_offset": 0,
      "kind_offset": 1,
      "ack_values": [
        1
      ],
      "error_offset": 2,
      "error_names": {
        "0x01": "Invalid date, or a counter out of range",
        "0x02": "Device type error — the device type byte is incorrect",
        "0x03": "Wrong request — an incorrect password was present",
        "0x04": "Error length",
        "0x05": "EEPROM write error",
        "0x07": "Data error"
      },
      "note": "ack_values is [1] and not [1, 2]. The manual documents C/R/A 0x02 as an 'Acknowledge frame' but never shows one, while all five worked examples answer with 0x01 — and the answer tables that print 0 are copy-paste errors the research identified. Matching a frame kind the vendor never demonstrated would close commands on evidence nobody has seen.",
      "payloads": {
        "0x28": {
          "description": "GET_ALARM_DATA answer. Each alarm date is day / month / year-2000 / 0x00 plain binary — NOT an epoch and NOT BCD. Recovered from nine captured frames (54 date fields, zero invalid) rather than from the manual, which types these Uint32_t 'dd/mm/yy' and never says how that packs. A date of 0/0/2000 means the alarm has never fired.",
